cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
title: "Can white holes merge? Eardley lifetimes of white holes in binaries (paper and interactive 3D simulation)"
abstract: >-
  A short paper and an interactive browser simulation. Using the exact
  thin-null-shell model of Barcelo, Carballo-Rubio and Garay, the paper gives
  the lifetime of a white hole of mass M hit by a perturbation dM in closed
  form, tau_E = 4M[ln(M/dM) - 1 - dM/M] (G = c = 1), shows that the energy of a
  late explosion follows the universal profile 1 - exp((dt - tau_E)/4M), with
  the rest left behind as a black-hole remnant. The cosmic microwave
  background alone limits tau_E to at most about 250-410 M for masses from
  10 to 6.5e9 solar masses, and a single quantum to at most about 880 M.
  Compared with gravitational-wave inspiral, a white hole reaches merger only
  if first exposed at a separation of about 6-10 M or less, within the last
  few orbits; a first explosion leaves the partner only 12-50 of its own mass
  units to explode before it is sealed; with the time-reversed no-bifurcation
  theorem, white holes in binaries either explode, leaving black-hole
  remnants, or merge as black holes. The 3D simulation shows an isolated white hole and white-hole pair
  and white-hole plus black-hole collision modes timed by the paper's formula.
  All numbers are reproduced by paper/compute.py. English and Turkish
  interfaces.
